---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Concept — Case Study Layout (Pack)</title>
    <style is:global>
      * {
        box-sizing: border-box;
      }

      body {
        margin: 0;
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        color: #171717;
        background: #fff;
        line-height: 1.6;
      }

      .top-bar {
        position: sticky;
        top: 0;
        background: #fff;
        border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;
        padding: 1rem 2rem;
        font-size: 0.8rem;
        color: #888;
        z-index: 10;
      }

      .col {
        max-width: 680px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 0 2rem;
      }

      .wide {
        max-width: 980px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 0 2rem;
      }

      .placeholder {
        background: #e4e4e4;
        border: 1px dashed #bbb;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        color: #888;
        font-size: 0.7rem;
        font-family: ui-monospace, monospace;
        text-align: center;
        padding: 1rem;
        border-radius: 2px;
      }

      /* Hero */
      .hero {
        padding: 5rem 0 3rem;
      }

      .hero .eyebrow {
        font-size: 0.8rem;
        color: #888;
        margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
      }

      .hero h1 {
        font-size: 3rem;
        font-weight: 500;
        margin: 0 0 1rem;
        letter-spacing: -0.02em;
      }

      .hero .summary {
        font-size: 1.25rem;
        color: #444;
        max-width: 46ch;
        margin: 0 0 2rem;
      }

      .meta {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
        gap: 1rem;
        font-size: 0.85rem;
        padding: 1.5rem 0;
        border-top: 1px solid #eee;
      }

      .meta div span {
        display: block;
        color: #888;
        font-size: 0.75rem;
        margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
      }

      .hero-media {
        height: 460px;
        margin-top: 2rem;
      }

      /* Section rhythm */
      section {
        padding: 4.5rem 0;
      }

      section + section {
        border-top: 1px solid #eee;
      }

      .section-label {
        font-size: 0.75rem;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
        color: #888;
        margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
      }

      section h2 {
        font-size: 1.9rem;
        font-weight: 500;
        margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
        letter-spacing: -0.01em;
      }

      section p {
        margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
        color: #333;
      }

      section p:last-child {
        margin-bottom: 0;
      }

      /* Solution sub-blocks: alternating media/text */
      .solution-block {
        display: flex;
        gap: 3rem;
        align-items: center;
        margin-bottom: 4rem;
      }

      .solution-block:last-child {
        margin-bottom: 0;
      }

      .solution-block.reverse {
        flex-direction: row-reverse;
      }

      .solution-block .media {
        flex: 1.1;
        height: 320px;
      }

      .solution-block .text {
        flex: 1;
      }

      .solution-block h3 {
        font-size: 1.3rem;
        font-weight: 500;
        margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
      }

      .solution-block .tag {
        display: inline-block;
        font-size: 0.7rem;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.06em;
        color: #888;
        margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
      }

      /* Secondary / lighter section */
      .secondary-grid {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
        gap: 1.5rem;
        margin-top: 2rem;
      }

      .secondary-grid .placeholder {
        height: 180px;
      }

      /* Outcome */
      .outcome {
        background: #fafafa;
      }

      /* Footer nav */
      .footer-nav {
        display: flex;
        justify-content: space-between;
        align-items: center;
        padding: 3rem 2rem;
        border-top: 1px solid #eee;
        font-size: 0.9rem;
      }

      .quote-slot {
        border-left: 2px solid #ddd;
        padding-left: 1.25rem;
        font-size: 1.1rem;
        color: #444;
        font-style: italic;
        margin: 2rem 0;
      }

      .confirm {
        color: #b45309;
        background: #fff7ed;
        border: 1px solid #fed7aa;
        padding: 0.15rem 0.4rem;
        border-radius: 3px;
        font-size: 0.85em;
        font-style: normal;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="top-bar">Concept — Case Study Layout (Pack) · wireframe, content positioning only</div>

    <!-- HERO -->
    <div class="col hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Case Study</p>
      <h1>Pack</h1>
      <p class="summary">
        Turning an agency's custom commerce builds into a real product, without losing what
        made those builds good.
      </p>
      <div class="meta">
        <div><span>Company</span>Pack</div>
        <div><span>Role</span>Lead Designer &amp; Design Director, Nessa Labs</div>
        <div><span>Timeframe</span><span class="confirm">confirm dates</span></div>
        <div><span>Team</span><span class="confirm">confirm team size</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="wide hero-media placeholder">01-hero-a.webp / 01-hero-b.webp — full-bleed hero shot</div>

    <!-- CHALLENGE -->
    <section>
      <div class="col">
        <p class="section-label">Challenge</p>
        <h2>From bespoke builds to a real product</h2>
        <p>
          Pack started as an agency, building fully custom headless commerce sites for brands
          like Cuts and Vuori. That work was good precisely because it was custom: a small
          team hand-building one storefront at a time, solving each brand's problems directly.
        </p>
        <p>
          After closing their seed round, Pack needed to become a product. The same team, the
          same expertise, but now serving hundreds of merchants instead of a handful of
          clients. That's a different design problem than it sounds like. The tools that make
          one-off custom work great don't automatically make sense as something a stranger can
          pick up and use without you standing over their shoulder.
        </p>
        <p>
          I led design on this transition, working directly with Pack's product and
          engineering team, including CEO Cory Cummings, to figure out what "productized"
          actually meant here. Two things mattered most: the site customizer had to give
          marketers real creative control without turning into another clunky page builder,
          and the dashboard and merchandising tools had to make sense to store owners who'd
          never touched a headless stack before.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="col" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <div class="placeholder" style="height: 280px;">
          03-s01-transition-desktop.webp — agency-to-product framing visual
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- APPROACH -->
    <section>
      <div class="col">
        <p class="section-label">Approach</p>
        <h2>Interviews before screens</h2>
        <p>
          We started with interviews, not screens. My team and I talked to merchants who'd
          tried other headless platforms to find out where they actually got stuck, not where
          we assumed they would. A few patterns showed up fast: onboarding took too long,
          merchandising tools were an afterthought bolted onto commerce infrastructure, and
          every customizer we looked at asked marketers to think like developers.
        </p>
        <p>
          That research shaped a hard call early on: build less, but build it right. Rather
          than chase feature parity with every competitor, we prioritized the handful of
          workflows that would make or break someone's first week on the platform. Everything
          else could come later.
        </p>
        <p>
          From there it was the usual rhythm of a small, fast-moving design team: sketches
          into clickable prototypes, testing rough ideas with Pack's team before committing
          engineering time, then refining.
          <span class="confirm">confirm: any process detail worth naming here?</span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SOLUTION -->
    <section>
      <div class="wide">
        <p class="section-label">Solution</p>
        <h2 style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">Three problems worth solving well</h2>

        <div class="solution-block">
          <div class="media placeholder">
            12-s02-customizer-sidebar-desktop.webp<br />13-s02-realtime-editing-desktop.webp
          </div>
          <div class="text">
            <p class="tag">01 — The site customizer</p>
            <h3>Real editing, not an abstraction of it</h3>
            <p>
              Most WYSIWYG site builders make you choose between flexibility and simplicity,
              usually failing at both. We rebuilt the customizer around a sidebar that slides
              out over a live preview of the actual storefront, so a marketer edits the real
              page, not an abstraction of it.
            </p>
            <p>
              The goal was letting a marketer launch a new landing page or run an A/B test
              without pulling in a developer. That's the actual measure of whether a
              customizer works.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="solution-block reverse">
          <div class="media placeholder">
            04-s01-dashboard-customization-desktop.webp<br />05-s01-dashboard-analytics-desktop.webp
          </div>
          <div class="text">
            <p class="tag">02 — The dashboard</p>
            <h3>One place to understand the store</h3>
            <p>
              Store owners needed one place to understand how their storefront was actually
              performing: revenue, traffic, conversion, site speed. Rather than a wall of
              charts, we organized the dashboard around what a merchant would want to check on
              a given day, with a sidebar that carries across multiple storefronts.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="solution-block">
          <div class="media placeholder">
            06-s01-bundling-desktop.webp<br />07-s01-collection-building-desktop.webp
          </div>
          <div class="text">
            <p class="tag">03 — Merchandising</p>
            <h3>Bundles in a few clicks, not a spreadsheet</h3>
            <p>
              Headless builds have a reputation for clunky merchandising, mostly because
              commerce infrastructure and merchandising tools get designed separately and
              bolted together. We redesigned this so merchants could build product bundles and
              collections through intelligent, attribute-based grouping instead of manual,
              one-by-one setup.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECONDARY / LIGHTER COVERAGE -->
    <section>
      <div class="col">
        <p class="section-label">Also shipped</p>
        <h2 style="font-size: 1.4rem;">Design system &amp; mobile access</h2>
        <p>
          We also shipped a global design system (custom iconography, a full component
          library) so Pack's design and engineering teams could keep building consistently
          after we handed off, and made sure the core merchant workflows worked properly on
          mobile, since a lot of that work happens on the go, not at a desk.
        </p>
        <div class="secondary-grid">
          <div class="placeholder">20-s04-iconography-desktop.webp</div>
          <div class="placeholder">18-s03-mobile-nav-desktop.webp</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- QUOTE SLOT -->
    <section>
      <div class="col">
        <p class="section-label">Quote (placement to test — wording pending)</p>
        <blockquote class="quote-slot">
          "The new Pack platform gives brands and agencies powerful front-end tools to
          communicate with customers more easily..."
          <span class="confirm">reattribution pending</span>
          <br />— Cory Cummings, CEO at Pack
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- OUTCOME -->
    <section class="outcome">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="section-label">Outcome</p>
        <h2 style="font-size: 1.4rem;">
          <span class="confirm">confirm: qualitative outcome, or skip this section entirely</span>
        </h2>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHAT I'D DO DIFFERENTLY (optional) -->
    <section>
      <div class="col">
        <p class="section-label">What I'd do differently (optional)</p>
        <p><span class="confirm">confirm: include or cut</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="footer-nav">
      <span>← Back to Work</span>
      <span>Next case study →</span>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
